## Tighten input validation on account settings pages

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
#81

### Summary
The account settings pages under `Areas/Identity/Pages/Account/Manage` (Index, Email,
ChangePassword, SetPassword) rely on the default Identity scaffolding's baseline data-annotation
attributes, which are loose enough to let malformed values through or to only reject them with a
generic message after a full round-trip. Tighten validation on every input field on these four
pages — phone number, display name, preferred color, new email, and new/confirm password — so
users get accurate client-side feedback, and so the values that do reach the database are safe and
well-formed.

### Motivation
Today: `[Phone]` accepts almost any digit/punctuation string; the password fields only enforce a
6-100 character length even though `IdentityConfiguration.cs` actually requires digit+upper+lower
server-side (via `UserManager`), so the page never tells the user the real rule until after
submit; Display Name has no character restriction beyond length, and it's rendered elsewhere in
the app (event pages, chat); a duplicate email currently surfaces whatever generic error
`ChangeEmailAsync`/`SetEmailAsync` happens to produce. This closes those gaps and gives the
account pages the same level of scrutiny as the rest of the app's user-facing forms.

---

### Scope
The four Manage pages named in the issue — `Index.cshtml(.cs)`, `Email.cshtml(.cs)`,
`ChangePassword.cshtml(.cs)`, `SetPassword.cshtml(.cs)` — plus, per an explicit follow-up request to
keep validation consistent site-wide, every other `Areas/Identity/Pages/Account` page that
duplicates one of these same fields/rules in its own `InputModel`: `Register.cshtml(.cs)`
(Password, DisplayName, Color), `ResetPassword.cshtml(.cs)` (Password), and
`ExternalLogin.cshtml(.cs)` (DisplayName — its own XML doc comment already claimed to match
Register's rule, which made the drift a real bug, not just an inconsistency). `Login.cshtml(.cs)`
and `DeletePersonalData.cshtml(.cs)` were checked and intentionally left alone — both only verify
an *existing* password (`[Required]` + `[DataType(Password)]`, no length/complexity attribute),
where none of this pass's new rules apply.

### Proposed Changes

#### Password policy (ChangePassword, SetPassword)
Strengthened, not just surfaced — a real policy change:
- `IdentityConfiguration.cs`: `RequiredLength` raised from 6 to 8, `RequireNonAlphanumeric` turned
  on (was `false`). `RequireDigit`/`RequireUppercase`/`RequireLowercase` stay `true`. This is
  enforced server-side by `UserManager` on every `ChangePasswordAsync`/`AddPasswordAsync` call
  regardless of what the page-level attributes say, so it's the actual source of truth.
- `InputModel.NewPassword` on both pages: replace the bare `[StringLength(100, MinimumLength = 6)]`
  with validation matching the new policy — minimum 8 characters, and a check that the value
  contains at least one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, one digit, and one symbol. Needs a
  custom `ValidationAttribute` (or an equivalent regex-based `[RegularExpression]`) since a single
  built-in attribute can't express "all four character classes present" — implementation detail to
  settle while building, but it must render via ASP.NET's unobtrusive client-side validation
  (`data-val-*` attributes) so the mismatch is caught before submit, not just server-side.
  Existing passwords set before this change keep working for sign-in; the stronger rule only
  applies the next time a password is set or changed.
- Confirm-password `[Compare]` behavior is unchanged — already works correctly per the issue;
  verify the mismatch message is visibly rendered in the UI (add a validation summary/message
  span next to the field if the current view is missing one).

#### Phone number (Index)
- Replace `[Phone]` with an E.164-based format: optional leading `+`, followed by 7-15 digits, no
  other characters. Reject anything else (letters, extra punctuation, spaces embedded mid-number,
  more than one `+`).
- Field stays optional (no `[Required]` added) — empty/null continues to mean "no phone number on
  file," matching today's behavior.

#### Display Name (Index)
- Keep `[Required]` + existing length rule (`ModelConstants.ApplicationUserDisplayNameMaxLength`).
- Add a check that blocks C0/C1 control characters (Unicode category Cc, e.g. U+0000-U+001F,
  U+007F-U+009F) and zero-width/invisible Unicode characters (e.g. U+200B zero-width space,
  U+200E/U+200F directional marks, U+FEFF byte-order mark) — broad Unicode letters/scripts
  otherwise remain allowed, so non-Latin display names keep working.
- Trim leading/trailing whitespace before validating and before persisting, and reject a name that
  is empty or all-whitespace after trimming (closes the gap where `[Required]` alone lets
  whitespace-only values through).

#### Preferred Color (Index)
No change — `[Required]` + `[RegularExpression(ModelConstants.HexColorPattern)]` +
`[StringLength(ModelConstants.HexColorLength, ...)]` already fully constrain this field to exactly
6 hex characters; issue only asked to confirm it's surfaced clearly, which it already is via
standard unobtrusive validation.

#### New email (Email)
- Keep `[Required]` + `[EmailAddress]`.
- On `OnPostChangeEmailAsync`, when the target email is already registered to another account,
  detect that explicitly (e.g. via `_userManager.FindByEmailAsync`) and add a specific
  `ModelState` error (e.g. "This email address is already registered to another account.") instead
  of letting a generic Identity error surface. This is a deliberate choice to favor clear UX
  feedback over hiding account existence — accepted email-enumeration tradeoff, see Out of Scope.

#### Client-side validation
All new/changed rules above must participate in ASP.NET Core's existing unobtrusive client-side
validation (the same `jquery.validate` + `jquery.validate.unobtrusive` pipeline already wired into
these pages) so invalid input is flagged before a round-trip, not only after. A custom password
ValidationAttribute needs a matching client-side adapter registered in `site.js` (or an inline
script on the two password pages) to produce the same before-submit feedback as the built-in
attributes.

#### Site-wide consistency pass (Register, ResetPassword, ExternalLogin)
Following an explicit request to make sure nothing elsewhere broke or drifted once the rules above
landed:
- `Register.cshtml.cs` — `Password` gets the same `ModelConstants.PasswordMinLength`/
  `PasswordComplexityPattern` rule as ChangePassword/SetPassword/ResetPassword (was still the old
  bare 6-100 length check, which would pass client-side and then fail server-side against the
  now-stricter `IdentityConfiguration` policy with a confusing generic error); `DisplayName` gets
  `ModelConstants.DisplayNameContentPattern` and is trimmed before persisting, matching
  Manage/Index; `Color` switches from a hardcoded inline `[RegularExpression(@"^[A-Fa-f0-9]{6}$")]`
  to `ModelConstants.HexColorPattern` (plus the matching `[StringLength]`), removing a second
  hand-copied pattern that could silently drift from the shared constant.
- `ResetPassword.cshtml.cs` — `Password` gets the same fix as Register's, for the same reason (it
  had the identical stale 6-100 rule).
- `ExternalLogin.cshtml.cs` — `DisplayName` gets `ModelConstants.DisplayNameContentPattern` and is
  trimmed before persisting; its `Color` field already used `ModelConstants.HexColorPattern`, so no
  change there.
- `DevelopmentDataSeeder.cs`'s hardcoded dev-seed password (`"Dev123"`, 6 characters, no symbol) no
  longer satisfies the strengthened `IdentityConfiguration` policy and broke every
  `WebApplicationFactory`-based test that boots the app in Development — updated to `"Dev123!@"`.
  This was caught by the full test suite while implementing the original four-page change, not by
  this follow-up request, but is recorded here since it's part of why the password policy change
  ripples site-wide.

#### Tests
`WhenWorksWeb.Tests` coverage for the new/changed validation rules on the original four Manage
pages: valid and invalid cases for the phone regex, the password complexity attribute, the Display
Name character/whitespace rule, and the Email duplicate-detection path. Extended, for the site-wide
consistency pass, with matching coverage for `RegisterModel.InputModel`'s Password/DisplayName/
Color, `ResetPasswordModel.InputModel.Password`, and `ExternalLoginModel.InputModel.DisplayName`.
Per CLAUDE.md, tests are only added here because explicitly requested for this task.

---

### Acceptance Criteria
- `IdentityConfiguration.cs` requires passwords of at least 8 characters containing uppercase,
  lowercase, digit, and symbol; `ChangePassword`/`SetPassword` reject a non-complying `NewPassword`
  client-side (before submit) with a message describing the actual rule, and server-side
  regardless of client-side bypass.
- Index page's Phone number field rejects any value that isn't empty or a valid E.164-shaped
  number (optional `+`, 7-15 digits); valid international numbers are accepted.
- Index page's Display Name field rejects control characters and zero-width/invisible Unicode
  characters, rejects empty-after-trim values, and persists the trimmed value — while still
  accepting non-Latin scripts and ordinary punctuation/spacing.
- Email page's "already registered" case produces a specific, clear `ModelState` error rather than
  a generic failure message.
- Confirm-password mismatch message is visibly rendered on both ChangePassword and SetPassword.
- `Register.cshtml`, `ResetPassword.cshtml`, and `ExternalLogin.cshtml` enforce the same
  Password/DisplayName/Color rules as the Manage pages for the fields they each collect, so no
  page in `Areas/Identity/Pages/Account` accepts a value another page in the same area would
  reject.
- All of the above have matching xUnit test coverage in `WhenWorksWeb.Tests`.

### Out of Scope
- `Login.cshtml` and `DeletePersonalData.cshtml` — both only verify an existing password, which
  none of these new rules apply to (see Scope above).
- Rate limiting / throttling on the change-password or change-email POST handlers, lockout policy
  tuning, or any other anti-automation hardening — separate concern from field-level validation;
  would need its own discussion and spec if pursued.
- Accepting the email-enumeration tradeoff that comes with a specific "already registered" message
  on the Email page (chosen deliberately for UX clarity — see Proposed Changes) — revisiting that
  tradeoff is out of scope for this pass.
- Any change to `ModelConstants.HexColorPattern`/Preferred Color validation — already sufficient.
- Migrating existing users' passwords to meet the new complexity policy — enforcement only applies
  going forward, on the next password set/change.
